WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.700
Is Ledgerly worth building, and who is it for?

2
00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:03.450
Morgan, its fictional founder, asks Viable.

3
00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:07.013
Morgan opens Ledgerly's workspace in Viable.

4
00:00:07.063 --> 00:00:10.256
One question stays in view: is this product
viable?

5
00:00:10.306 --> 00:00:14.329
It starts with product truth: what Ledgerly
actually does,

6
00:00:14.379 --> 00:00:18.402
and what it does not. Every later conclusion
builds on this.

7
00:00:18.452 --> 00:00:21.587
Evidence is recorded and reviewed: owner
interviews,

8
00:00:21.637 --> 00:00:24.772
private beta usage, and pricing objections.

9
00:00:24.822 --> 00:00:27.619
A claim is approved only when reviewed
evidence supports it.

10
00:00:27.669 --> 00:00:30.467
This one is held back: there is no outcome
evidence yet.

11
00:00:30.517 --> 00:00:35.494
Who is it for? Two hypotheses: design studios,
or solo freelancers.

12
00:00:35.544 --> 00:00:39.643
Each dimension is rated with its evidence and
confidence. No hidden score.

13
00:00:39.693 --> 00:00:44.421
New market evidence arrives: a competitor
announcement.

14
00:00:44.471 --> 00:00:49.200
It stays a suggestion until Morgan reviews it
by name.

15
00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:55.047
Now the core question: is Ledgerly marketable
to design studios?

16
00:00:55.097 --> 00:01:00.894
Morgan picks up the saved marketability
assessment.

17
00:01:00.944 --> 00:01:08.089
Proof: one reviewed usage figure and no case
study yet. Rated 2 of 4.

18
00:01:08.139 --> 00:01:11.376
The answer is explained, not scored: strong
product truth and audience,

19
00:01:11.426 --> 00:01:14.664
weak offer, reach and sales readiness.

20
00:01:14.714 --> 00:01:19.163
Each gap can become an owned next step.

21
00:01:19.213 --> 00:01:23.663
The offer gap is now an open action with a
named owner.

22
00:01:23.713 --> 00:01:24.663
Viable: find out whether your product is worth
building,

23
00:01:24.713 --> 00:01:25.663
for whom, and what to do next.

